      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hiseville was renamed in 1867 to honor Elijah Hise, a United States congressman who represented Kentucky.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiseville%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming Hiseville, the community was jokingly called Goosehorn because local women frequently raised geese.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hiseville_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Famed Kentucky longhunter and pioneer Henry Skaggs lived in Hiseville starting in 1801.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiseville%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hiseville developed along a historic stagecoach route that connected Nashville and Lexington during the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hiseville_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "During the mid-19th century, Hiseville thrived as a bustling hamlet featuring a steam mill, a hotel, and a distillery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hiseville_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Hiseville functioned as an incorporated city until it officially disincorporated in 2004.",
